# Stock /system/etc/init/wificond.rc, neutered: the service still EXISTS by name
# but execs a no-op, so starting it does nothing at all.
#
# WHY NOT JUST "disabled" -- THE PREVIOUS VERSION OF THIS FILE
#
# Both stock wificond and waydroid-wifid register the servicemanager name
# "wifinl80211", and addService() overwrites, so whichever registered last owns
# it.  The first attempt at closing that race added one word, "disabled", to the
# stock definition.  That was verified at the time and it was not enough.
#
# "disabled" means only "do not auto-start this at class main".  It does not
# stop anything from asking init to start the service later, and on this image
# something does: with the file in effect and confirmed by reading it from
# inside the container, getprop init.svc.wificond still reported "running", and
# wificond appeared within 1 ms of
#
#   WifiNl80211Manager: Setting up interface for client mode: wlan0
#
# So the on-demand start beat us every time, wificond took the name, and Android
# then talked to a wificond that answers "No wiphy is found" and fails the
# interface setup.  The reason Stages 2 and 3 never saw this is that only the
# connectivity-mode path Stage 4 reaches asks for the interface that way.
#
# Killing it in a loop is WORSE, not better: init restarts a dead service on a
# 5 s backoff, and a registered-then-dead binder makes Android report
# "Failed to get reference to wificond", which is a different and more confusing
# failure than the one being chased.
#
# Hence this shape.  Three deliberate parts:
#
#   /system/bin/true  execs and exits immediately, so it can never reach
#                     addService() and can never take the name.  It is a toybox
#                     symlink present in this image; checked, not assumed.
#   oneshot           init must NOT restart it when it exits.  Without this the
#                     no-op would respawn forever on the same 5 s backoff.
#   disabled          kept, so it is not started at class main either.
#
# The capabilities and supplementary groups are dropped with the real binary:
# /system/bin/true has no use for NET_RAW or NET_ADMIN, and a service definition
# should not carry privileges its executable does not need.
#
# Effect: waydroid-wifid owns "wifinl80211" from whenever it starts, and nothing
# ever overwrites it.  This removes the start-ordering requirement that the
# workaround needed (wificond first, daemon last) -- with this in place the
# daemon may start at any time.
#
# Reverting is deleting this file; that restores stock wificond, which will then
# fight the daemon for the name again.
#
# Deploy to /var/lib/waydroid/overlay/system/etc/init/wificond.rc (0644
# root:root).  Dropping the file in is NOT enough: the overlay lowerdir is
# already mounted, so it needs `systemctl restart waydroid-container.service`
# and a fresh session.  See AGENTS.md and 32-wifi-stage3.md.
#
# Original for comparison: artifacts/overlay/system/etc/init/wificond.rc.orig
# Evidence and full reasoning: docs/34-wifi-second-radio.md

service wificond /system/bin/true
    class main
    user wifi
    group wifi
    disabled
    oneshot
